.. meta::
   :template: blog.html
   :title: A Fast and Accurate(ish) Floating-point debugger
   :date: 2025-03-05

.. container:: banner

   This blog is an abridged version of `Pavel <https://pavpanchekha.com>`__ and
   my paper “Mixing Condition Numbers and Oracles for Accurate Floating-point
   Debugging”, which was accepted at `ARITH'25 <https://www.arith2025.org/>`__.

Floating-point numbers are how we squeeze the infinite world of
real numbers into a finite space on our computers. As programmers, we use them
all the time to mimic real-world calculations. But it turns out there are
consequences for approximating infinite numbers with finite ones. These
consequences trouble programmers in the form of rounding and over/underflow
errors among others.

Programmers debug non-numerical errors like runtime or memory bugs by tracking
down the cause, often with tools like ``gdb`` or ``valgrind``. But what about
numerical code? There are, in fact, two main classes of tools designed to help
programmers reason about floating-point error: floating-point static analyzers
(like, ``FPTaylor``) and floating-point debuggers (like, ``Herbgrind``). Static
analyzers look at the entire input space of a floating-point program and provide
(possibly sound) guarantees of its numerical error. Debuggers on the other hand
observe an actual execution of the program and warn programmers about potential
numerical errors.

Both tools have their own strengths and drawbacks. Static analyzers are accurate
but slow and debuggers are fast but inaccurate. Both of these approaches to
floating-point error detection cover each other's weaknesses pretty nicely.
Wouldn't it be nice to combine the advances in both of these approaches to come
up with a tool that is both fast and accurate?


